 4. Do not use OOS results as a process-tuning oracle.
 5. Preserve negative and inconclusive evidence.
 6. Do not claim a strategy delta worked until its expected effect is actually observed.
 7. When there is insufficient evidence, choose UNVERIFIED rather than forcing RETAIN or REVERT.
+
+## Process audit — frontier-first selector (P-001, agent 01, 2026-11-14)
+
+Question: does explicit highest-value focused-task selection improve information flow relative to the prior opportunistic queue-driven choice?
+
+Observed durable evidence:
+- Pre-delta: after the first real-data MA(20/60) rejection (`STATE.md` line 313), at least eight MA-crossover-specific activation records followed (STATE.md lines 358, 958, 999, 1028, 1117, 1178, 1234, plus the regime-feature diagnostic at 1331). No other signal class was executed before the mean-reversion cell (lines 495/547). These activations refined one already-rejected hypothesis; they also built the regime / universe / t-statistic diagnostics later reused as gates, so they were not pure duplication.
+- Post-delta: 7-8 activations closed 5-6 distinct signal-class cells (mean reversion, cross-sectional relative strength, volatility targeting, momentum, cross-sectional momentum, breakout) plus 2 momentum qualifications, with zero equivalent re-tests of a resolved cell. Five cells closed FALSIFIED, one SUPPORTED (momentum, admitted as candidate positive evidence), one boundary FALSIFIED.
+- The delta's expected effect (fewer equivalent experiments, more information per activation) is therefore observed in the record, not merely asserted.
+
+Confounds (recorded, not resolved):
+- The comparison is not controlled: the post-delta period also had a more mature gate stack, which lowered per-cell cost. The campaign one-task-per-session firewall postdates the delta and is not the cause.
+- "No equivalent re-tests" is partly tautological under a frontier-first rule, which selects distinct cells by construction. The non-tautological evidence is that the selected cells were decision-relevant and closed with decisive, independently verified verdicts.
+
+New binding constraint (next bottleneck):
+- The controller selector is a deterministic ordinal rank over the durable queue (`campaign_controller.py:206-225`, `269-383`). It ranks well within a populated choice set but cannot generate cells.
+- The open research queue is now 4/4 momentum-family qualifications (R-001 cost, R-002 hold period, R-003 volatility gate, R-005 sizing); no new signal class remains OPEN (R-004 resolved). Without replenishment, agents 02/04/06/08 will all refine the single admitted cell, reproducing "refining the most interesting recent result" at the queue level even though selection is now principled. Candidate task R-006 (fixed-pair relative-value spread) is queued to test replenishment.
+
+Record-completeness observation (minor, unresolved):
+- The "Recent learning history" table has no row for activation 37361000967 (it is described in the active-delta text and in `STATE.md` line 1390).
+- The cross-sectional-momentum cell (commit 8357e5fe) has an artifact and a frontier row (row 34) but no `STATE.md` section and no learning-history row.
+- The history table is therefore not a complete activation ledger; later agents must cross-check the frontier table and the artifacts. This weakens, but does not break, duplicate detection.
+
+Process decision: RETAIN the frontier-first selector; the binding constraint has moved from selection to queue replenishment.

