@@ -73,9 +73,9 @@
 Question: does explicit highest-value focused-task selection improve information flow relative to the prior opportunistic queue-driven choice?
 
 Observed durable evidence:
 - Pre-delta: after the first real-data MA(20/60) rejection (`STATE.md` line 313), at least eight MA-crossover-specific activation records followed (STATE.md lines 358, 958, 999, 1028, 1117, 1178, 1234, plus the regime-feature diagnostic at 1331). No other signal class was executed before the mean-reversion cell (lines 495/547). These activations refined one already-rejected hypothesis; they also built the regime / universe / t-statistic diagnostics later reused as gates, so they were not pure duplication.
-- Post-delta: 7-8 activations closed 5-6 distinct signal-class cells (mean reversion, cross-sectional relative strength, volatility targeting, momentum, cross-sectional momentum, breakout) plus 2 momentum qualifications, with zero equivalent re-tests of a resolved cell. Five cells closed FALSIFIED, one SUPPORTED (momentum, admitted as candidate positive evidence), one boundary FALSIFIED.
+- Post-delta: 8 activations closed 6 distinct signal-class cells (mean reversion, cross-sectional relative strength, volatility targeting, momentum, cross-sectional momentum, breakout) plus 2 momentum qualifications, with zero equivalent re-tests of a resolved cell. Five cells closed FALSIFIED, one SUPPORTED (momentum, admitted as candidate positive evidence).
 - The delta's expected effect (fewer equivalent experiments, more information per activation) is therefore observed in the record, not merely asserted.
 
 Confounds (recorded, not resolved):
 - The comparison is not controlled: the post-delta period also had a more mature gate stack, which lowered per-cell cost. The campaign one-task-per-session firewall postdates the delta and is not the cause.

